From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/imagination: force the firmware stop on hard reset

A hard reset is the driver's answer to a stuck GPU, and it never resets one.
pvr_power_reset() calls pvr_fw_hard_reset() only if pvr_power_fw_disable()
succeeded first (pvr_power.c:548-549). On a hard reset that is just
pvr_fw_stop(), which opens by polling ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE with a 1 s timeout
on cores without LAYOUT_MARS (pvr_fw_startstop.c:225-230). A stuck GPU does not
go idle, the poll times out, and

	if (err && hard_reset)
		goto err_device_lost;

(pvr_power.c:574) declares the device lost with nothing reset. This project's
earlier Armbian-based 6.18 port of this board measured exactly that on this SoC
and firmware: "FW stalled, trying hard reset" followed 1.008 s later by
"GPU device lost", one poll timeout.

Skipping the failed poll is not a fix. pvr_fw_stop() is also the only hardware
quiesce the driver has: the MTS DM_ASSOC clears, the BIF/SLC drain polls, and
the final ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET write. The hard reset path gates the GPU clocks
right after it (pvr_power_device_suspend() -> power_off). That earlier port's
first attempt ignored the error, skipped that quiesce, and was withdrawn as
unsafe: on this SoC a clock stopped under a busy block has already frozen the
interconnect once (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-pll-gpu0-must-be-running-before-the-gpu-binds`).

So the stop is forced, not skipped. __pvr_fw_stop(force) walks the same
sequence; in force mode each poll gets 10 ms instead of 1 s and a timeout is
recorded instead of returned. The core is then put into soft reset whether it
went idle or not, while its clocks still run, SOFT_RESET2 is asserted under the
same xe_tpu2 guard pvr_fw_start() uses, and both registers are read back to
fence the writes before the caller unclocks the block. A timeout is logged at
err level, "GPU did not go idle, forcing soft reset ...", so it reaches the
console. pvr_power_fw_disable() takes the forced path only when hard_reset is
set; the graceful stop used by runtime suspend is unchanged.

In force mode the META TxVECINT_BHALT read is skipped: pvr_meta_cr_read32()
polls its slave port with its own 1 s timeout (pvr_fw_meta.c:41-61), which the
short leash above would not cover. Without that read the Garten poll runs, on
the same 10 ms.

The same forced stop replaces the graceful one on the boot-failure path of
pvr_power_fw_enable(). A firmware that did not come up there has nothing to
drain, and the graceful stop would give up after its first 1 s poll without
writing SOFT_RESET, before pvr_power_device_resume() gates the clocks
(err_power_off). A firmware left unstarted on a resume runs exactly that path
(exercised by a test that skips the firmware start on resume).

Differences from the earlier port's equivalent patch, which was written against
an older driver: the LAYOUT_MARS branch (MARS_IDLE) that mainline added is
covered too; the repeated error handling is one helper; and, deliberately, the
graceful path gets no new writes. That port also asserted SOFT_RESET2 and fenced both
registers on every runtime suspend. Here the graceful stop only runs after the
core reported idle, and the reset line is asserted before the gates, so that
change is not taken without a measurement of its own.
That patch ran a 902 s soak on the earlier port's A733 board with one firmware
stall: forced stop fired, "GPU device lost" did not follow, and the board kept
rendering. That was a different kernel; it says the sequence works on this
silicon, not that this port does.

Not established: the LAYOUT_MARS value of BXM-4-64 MC1. It comes from the
firmware header (pvr_fw.c:187) and was not decoded; the 1.008 s gap points to
the SIDEKICK branch.

The code touched here is byte-identical to mainline master (compared
2026-09-10).

Verified: applies with --fuzz=0 after `drm-imagination-run-the-device-lost-unplug-from-a-worker`; compile gate green. HW-proven on
the test board 2026-09-10 against a faked stall -- the first idle poll forced
to time out -- under real GL load: 3 of 3 resets take the forced stop and
render correctly afterwards; without this patch the same stimulus ends in
"GPU device lost". A genuinely wedged bus is not covered.

Falsifier on the test board needs a real firmware stall, which an injection cannot fake:
"FW stalled, trying hard reset", then "GPU did not go idle, forcing soft
reset", and no "GPU device lost"; a GL acceptance run passes in the same boot.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_fw_startstop.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_fw_startstop.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include "pvr_rogue_meta.h"
 #include "pvr_vm.h"
 
+#include <drm/drm_print.h>
 #include <linux/compiler.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/ktime.h>
@@ -16,6 +17,13 @@
 
 #define POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC 1000000
 
+/*
+ * A forced stop runs on a GPU that did not go idle. Each drain poll still gets
+ * a short chance to settle, but seven polls at POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC would hold the
+ * recovery for seven seconds to learn what the caller already knows.
+ */
+#define FORCE_POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC 10000
+
 static void
 rogue_axi_ace_list_init(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev)
 {
@@ -193,22 +201,53 @@
 	return err;
 }
 
-/**
- * pvr_fw_stop() - Stop FW processor
- * @pvr_dev: Target PowerVR device.
+/*
+ * pvr_fw_stop_poll_result() - Decide what a failed idle poll means
  *
- * Returns:
- *  * 0 on success, or
- *  * Any error returned by pvr_cr_poll_reg32().
+ * A graceful stop gives up on the first poll that times out. A forced stop
+ * records it and carries on, because the soft reset at the end is the point.
  */
-int
-pvr_fw_stop(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev)
+static int
+pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(int err, bool force, bool *not_idle)
+{
+	if (err && force) {
+		*not_idle = true;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	return err;
+}
+
+/*
+ * pvr_fw_stop_fence_reset() - Finish a forced soft reset before the clocks go
+ *
+ * The caller gates the GPU clocks next. A reset write still in the SOCIF
+ * pipeline when its clock stops leaves a live core behind, so assert
+ * SOFT_RESET2 as pvr_fw_start() does and read both registers back.
+ */
+static void
+pvr_fw_stop_fence_reset(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev)
+{
+	bool has_reset2 = PVR_HAS_FEATURE(pvr_dev, xe_tpu2);
+
+	if (has_reset2)
+		pvr_cr_write64(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET2, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET2_MASKFULL);
+
+	(void)pvr_cr_read64(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET);
+	if (has_reset2)
+		(void)pvr_cr_read64(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET2);
+}
+
+static int
+__pvr_fw_stop(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev, bool force)
 {
 	const u32 sidekick_idle_mask = ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE_MASKFULL &
 				       ~(ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE_GARTEN_EN |
 					 ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE_SOCIF_EN |
 					 ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE_HOSTIF_EN);
+	const u64 poll_timeout = force ? FORCE_POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC : POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC;
 	bool skip_garten_idle = false;
+	bool not_idle = false;
 	u64 layout_mars_value = 0;
 	bool layout_mars = false;
 	bool meta_fw = pvr_dev->fw_dev.processor_type == PVR_FW_PROCESSOR_TYPE_META;
@@ -225,13 +264,15 @@
 	if (!layout_mars) {
 		/* Wait for Sidekick/Jones to signal IDLE except for the Garten Wrapper. */
 		err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE, sidekick_idle_mask,
-					sidekick_idle_mask, POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+					sidekick_idle_mask, poll_timeout);
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 
 		/* Wait for SLC to signal IDLE. */
 		err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE, ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE_MASKFULL,
-					ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE_MASKFULL, POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+					ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE_MASKFULL, poll_timeout);
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 	}
@@ -256,33 +297,38 @@
 	/* Extra Idle checks. */
 	err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_BIF_STATUS_MMU, 0,
 				ROGUE_CR_BIF_STATUS_MMU_MASKFULL,
-				POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+				poll_timeout);
+	err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
 	err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_BIFPM_STATUS_MMU, 0,
 				ROGUE_CR_BIFPM_STATUS_MMU_MASKFULL,
-				POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+				poll_timeout);
+	err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
 	if (!PVR_HAS_FEATURE(pvr_dev, xt_top_infrastructure)) {
 		err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_BIF_READS_EXT_STATUS, 0,
 					ROGUE_CR_BIF_READS_EXT_STATUS_MASKFULL,
-					POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+					poll_timeout);
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 	}
 
 	err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_BIFPM_READS_EXT_STATUS, 0,
 				ROGUE_CR_BIFPM_READS_EXT_STATUS_MASKFULL,
-				POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+				poll_timeout);
+	err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
 	err = pvr_cr_poll_reg64(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SLC_STATUS1, 0,
 				ROGUE_CR_SLC_STATUS1_MASKFULL,
-				POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+				poll_timeout);
+	err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
@@ -294,18 +340,25 @@
 		/* Wait for SLC to signal IDLE. */
 		err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE,
 					ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE_MASKFULL,
-					ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE_MASKFULL, POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+					ROGUE_CR_SLC_IDLE_MASKFULL, poll_timeout);
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 
 		/* Wait for Sidekick/Jones to signal IDLE except for the Garten Wrapper. */
 		err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE, sidekick_idle_mask,
-					sidekick_idle_mask, POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+					sidekick_idle_mask, poll_timeout);
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 	}
 
-	if (meta_fw) {
+	/*
+	 * A forced stop skips this read: pvr_meta_cr_read32() polls the META slave
+	 * port with its own POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC, which the short leash cannot cover.
+	 * The Garten poll below then runs on that leash instead.
+	 */
+	if (meta_fw && !force) {
 		err = pvr_meta_cr_read32(pvr_dev, META_CR_TxVECINT_BHALT, &reg_value);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
@@ -324,7 +377,8 @@
 			err = pvr_cr_poll_reg32(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE,
 						ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE_GARTEN_EN,
 						ROGUE_CR_SIDEKICK_IDLE_GARTEN_EN,
-						POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
+						poll_timeout);
+			err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 			if (err)
 				return err;
 		}
@@ -339,17 +393,56 @@
 					ROGUE_CR_MARS_IDLE_MH_SYSARB0_EN,
 					ROGUE_CR_MARS_IDLE_CPU_EN |
 					ROGUE_CR_MARS_IDLE_MH_SYSARB0_EN,
-					POLL_TIMEOUT_USEC);
-
+					poll_timeout);
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_poll_result(err, force, &not_idle);
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 	}
 
+	if (not_idle)
+		drm_err(from_pvr_device(pvr_dev),
+			"GPU did not go idle, forcing soft reset with transactions possibly in flight\n");
+
 	if (PVR_HAS_FEATURE(pvr_dev, pbe2_in_xe))
 		pvr_cr_write64(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET,
 			       ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET__PBE2_XE__MASKFULL);
 	else
 		pvr_cr_write64(pvr_dev, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET, ROGUE_CR_SOFT_RESET_MASKFULL);
 
+	if (force)
+		pvr_fw_stop_fence_reset(pvr_dev);
+
 	return 0;
 }
+
+/**
+ * pvr_fw_stop() - Stop FW processor
+ * @pvr_dev: Target PowerVR device.
+ *
+ * Returns:
+ *  * 0 on success, or
+ *  * Any error returned by pvr_cr_poll_reg32().
+ */
+int
+pvr_fw_stop(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev)
+{
+	return __pvr_fw_stop(pvr_dev, false);
+}
+
+/**
+ * pvr_fw_stop_force() - Stop FW processor on a GPU that will not go idle
+ * @pvr_dev: Target PowerVR device.
+ *
+ * Walks the same drain sequence as pvr_fw_stop() on a short timeout and puts
+ * the core into soft reset whether or not it went idle, while its clocks still
+ * run. For the hard reset path, where the graceful stop can only time out, and
+ * for a firmware that failed to boot.
+ *
+ * Returns:
+ *  * 0 always; a poll that times out is logged, not returned.
+ */
+int
+pvr_fw_stop_force(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev)
+{
+	return __pvr_fw_stop(pvr_dev, true);
+}
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_fw_startstop.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_fw_startstop.h
@@ -9,5 +9,6 @@
 
 int pvr_fw_start(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev);
 int pvr_fw_stop(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev);
+int pvr_fw_stop_force(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev);
 
 #endif /* PVR_FW_STARTSTOP_H */
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c
@@ -126,7 +126,16 @@
 		disable_irq(pvr_dev->irq);
 	}
 
-	err = pvr_fw_stop(pvr_dev);
+	/*
+	 * A hard reset answers a GPU that is already stuck. The graceful stop
+	 * waits for idle first and can only time out there, which sends
+	 * pvr_power_reset() to "device lost" with nothing reset.
+	 */
+	if (hard_reset)
+		err = pvr_fw_stop_force(pvr_dev);
+	else
+		err = pvr_fw_stop(pvr_dev);
+
 	if (err && rpm_suspend)
 		enable_irq(pvr_dev->irq);
 
@@ -148,7 +157,12 @@
 	err = pvr_wait_for_fw_boot(pvr_dev);
 	if (err) {
 		drm_err(from_pvr_device(pvr_dev), "Firmware failed to boot\n");
-		pvr_fw_stop(pvr_dev);
+		/*
+		 * Nothing booted, so nothing to drain. The graceful stop would give
+		 * up at its first 1 s poll without writing SOFT_RESET, and a resume
+		 * gates the clocks right after this returns.
+		 */
+		pvr_fw_stop_force(pvr_dev);
 		goto out;
 	}
 
